ifficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "891e3ecc-7d6f-5b4d-a28c-fc1c71a2a958" :question/math-academy-id "q-363823" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  select the correct options in the computations of the matrix $U$ below.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}=5$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\n\nNow, we compute the $1$st column of $U.$ It is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${u}_{1}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n|  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal.\n\nHence, we must find vectors $u=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}]^{T}$ such that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to \n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nChoosing ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ as free variables, the general solution is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n|  |  | ${x}_{3}$ |  |  |\n\nthat we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince the two linearly independent solutions are already mutually orthogonal, we only need to normalize them. Hence, we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$\n\n| | ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-7}} |  |  |  |\n|  | $0$ |  |  |  | | | ${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-8}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-9}} |  |  |  |\n|  | $1$ |  |  |  | |\n| --- | --- |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=5$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{3}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables.\n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we only need to normalize them. This gives the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408019 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162042 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6732620-fad1-5dd3-98c5-07ba59d0b286" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Factor in the SVD of a Rectangular Matrix: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1624144f-dc91-5236-84ee-18b96e8277af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407934} {:answer/id #uuid "1f0a89b3-42e1-5c0c-9f1d-976b0b80e3f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407939} {:answer/id #uuid "746511ed-c96a-5c0b-a954-73fab4f89071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407962}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1624144f-dc91-5236-84ee-18b96e8277af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407934} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ef2a40d-dd8f-5054-9101-9b4095ee0f14" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408004} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e47d3ea-4750-55d7-9ad9-f144dbee61aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 175921